11) GROUP BY
Definition
GROUP BY is used to group rows that have the same value in one or more columns. It is mainly used with aggregate functions.

Syntax
SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name;
Example

Count employees in each department.

SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department;

Find total salary of each department.

SELECT department, SUM(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department;

Find average salary of each city.

SELECT city, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY city;
GROUP BY with WHERE

WHERE filters rows before grouping.

SELECT department, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
WHERE salary > 50000
GROUP BY department;

GROUP BY with ORDER BY
SELECT department, COUNT(*) AS total_emp
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department
ORDER BY total_emp DESC;

Important Points
GROUP BY creates groups based on one or more columns.
It is mostly used with aggregate functions.
WHERE executes before GROUP BY.
Every selected column must either:
appear in GROUP BY, or
be inside an aggregate function.

            -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

12) HAVING
Definition
HAVING is used to filter grouped data after the GROUP BY clause.

Difference Between WHERE and HAVING
             WHERE	                                                                            HAVING
Filters rows	                                                                        Filters groups
Executes before GROUP BY                                                               	Executes after GROUP BY
Cannot use aggregate functions	                                                        Can use aggregate functions
Can be used without GROUP BY	                                                        Usually used with GROUP BY

Syntax
SELECT column_name, aggregate_function(column_name)
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name
HAVING condition;
Example

Departments having more than 5 employees.

SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY department
HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;

Cities whose average salary is greater than 50000.

SELECT city, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
GROUP BY city
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000;

WHERE + HAVING Together
SELECT department, AVG(salary)
FROM Employees
WHERE salary > 40000
GROUP BY department
HAVING AVG(salary) > 60000;

SQL Execution Order
FROM
↓
WHERE
↓
GROUP BY
↓
HAVING
↓
SELECT
↓
ORDER BY

Important Points
WHERE filters rows.
HAVING filters groups.
HAVING is mostly used with aggregate functions.
WHERE and HAVING can be used together.
For better compatibility across different databases, write:
HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000

instead of
HAVING avg_salary > 50000
because not all databases allow aliases inside HAVING.

